      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we need to identify the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in standard form:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1.\n\\]\nHere, the terms involving \\(y^2\\) are inverted to get \\(\\frac{y^2}{27}\\), and the terms involving \\(x^2\\) are inverted to get \\(\\frac{x^2}{11}\\). This means the semi-major axis length is the square root of \\(\\frac{27}{1}\\) (since the right term \\(y^2\\) is positive).\n\nSo, the length of the semi-major axis is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{1}} = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\nIn a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the vertices are located at points \\((0, \\pm a)\\), where \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis length.\n\nThe distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is the length of the line segment connecting two vertices. Since the vertices are located at \\((0, \\pm 3\\sqrt{3})\\), the distance between them is:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
